<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bangor-on-Dee</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bangor-is-y-coed, Bangor Is-y-Coed</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bangor-on-Dee (Bangor-is-y-coed) is a village on the River Dee in Wrexham county borough, north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the site of a large British monastery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> records that its monks attended a meeting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-canterbury">Augustine of Canterbury</a> around 602, and that about 1,200 of them were killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith of Northumbria</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chester">Battle of Chester</a> around 615.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bangor-on-dee.jpg" alt="Bangor-on-Dee" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Spicke01 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangor-on-Dee_High_Street.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
